{"authored" [{"question" "q-122287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [0,3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b52a1eb499e0f24a2c5e" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{(1+{x}^{2})}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x=2x\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{2})}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[2x\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{2})}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=2{x}^{2}+1$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+x)|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{2}{3}(3)^{3}+(3)]-[\\frac{2}{3}(0)^{3}+(0)] \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b52a1eb499e0f24a2c5e" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122287" "field" nil 